From 8d252bf9cf4ac27651eab27f53de08853002cff3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:17:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] demote the string literals registered under full
 memory tracking

With -e-acsl-full-mtracking, every global is registered as a block in
__e_acsl_globals_init, including the variables that represent string
literals. The memory tracking analysis, which demotes the literals that it
monitors to named arrays, is not used in that mode, so the literals stayed
literals and were printed as such, both in the registration and where they
are used. Their storage then depends on the compiler and the linker: clang
puts literals in a mergeable section, and GNU ld stores "," at the end of
" ,". The two registered blocks overlap, and \valid_read(" ," + 1) fails on
correct code.

Under full tracking, demote every registered string literal before the
injection. Demoting them one by one with Demote_string_literal.demote costs
one traversal of the AST per literal (98 s instead of 2 s for a file with
4000 literals), so add Demote_string_literal.demote_list, which demotes a
list of literals in one traversal and marks the AST as changed once.

Regression test: tests/full-mtracking/literal_strings.c. The tests compile
with gcc and -fno-merge-constants, so the run-time failure does not show
there; the generated code shows the literals as named arrays. The oracles
of concurrency/parallel_threads.c and concurrency/threads_debug.c, which
run with full tracking, change accordingly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
